hi there this is uh steve kaufman uh  i normally talk mostly about language  learning a little bit about education  and i'd like to talk a little bit today  about the importance of reading in  education  i came across an item on the internet  entitled academically adrift  and it was a study which showed  that 45 percent of students in  universities in the united states did  not demonstrate any significant  improvement in learning  during their first two years of college  36 percent of students did not  demonstrate any significant improvement  over four years of college and if we go  through this and even students who  improved improved on average only 0.18  standard deviations blah blah blah i  don't want i don't understand what all  of that means but they didn't improve  very much and as you read through the  article and i will try because you can't  post a link on youtube but i'll i'll  post it on my blog the linguist on  language  but you read through the article and it  really boils down to those courses where  students are asked to or forced to read  a lot they improve  and those courses where they don't read  so much they don't improve that to me  seems to be the bottom line here  so that was one article i should point  out by the way that um  i remember when i was at mcgill  university in my first year  the chancellor stood up and spoke to the  first year students and told them to  look at the fellow on the right or the  girl on the right and the person or the  person on the right the person on the  left  and one of them won't be here next year  because the first year one-third of  people fail out  so that's another thing about  universities a lot of people go there  probably without too clear of an idea  why they're there and a high percentage  of them fail out  given that universities cost  an awful lot of money i mean the average  cost for a university student  is something like  two or three times the cost of a student  in the regular school system  uh depending on where you are it's 20 30  40 000  of which the student pays a certain  amount themselves  but most of it is paid for by the  taxpayer or by in the case of private  universities if they have  scholarship funds i mean very often it's  paid by someone else or the parent  so one-third fail out those that stay  behind don't learn very much that's  really not very encouraging and the key  to  improving your learning is to read  so then  why does the university matter why don't  people just read more  uh  you know that would cost it wouldn't  cost thirty thousand dollars a year  uh  get yourself a reading list on subjects  that are of interest to you now of  course i know the answer  the answer is that people need the  ticket that the university provides  because that's some kind of a ticket to  a better job  and other people say that there's all  the interaction and and of course uh  stimulus and so forth yeah but according  to these statistics 30 40 of the  students don't learn very much so how  much stimulus is there at the university  i don't know  there was another series of articles in  canada  about an academic sort of  watchdog  canadian  group of of university professors who  were going around investigating the  religious based universities  and their view was that a university  that required you to  make some statement or commitment about  your faith or your belief your belief in  certain religious  uh dogma  therefore made the university less of a  of a place where free you know  academic activities could be  pursued  and i'm sure that's true to some extent  however  um if we bear in mind that many  universities in the world were started  as religious institutions  if we consider the degree to which  political correctness correctness  dominates our universities  uh i'm not convinced  that there is in fact less opportunity  for learning  at these religious institutions  besides which  if the main thing is how much you read  then it's really up to the student how  much they choose to read and no one can  prevent a student from reading whatever  they want to read  and if the main real reason why people  go to universities is to get a ticket  then you know if if i add to a job  then  you know if i'm an employer and i have a  student who graduated from the uh  you know holy cross seminary or from the  uh you know uh  some kind of a muslim or sikh or jewish  or buddhist  institution uh if i want that to  influence you know the validity that i  uh ascribe to that diploma well that'll  be my choice  so um  yeah i i don't think this this  group of professors should be out there  saying that this university because it's  a religious  institution therefore is less valid  um  i think it's going to be up to the  students as to what they make of it of  course the professors do influence the  students  uh and and that's also kind of  unfortunate uh we see that in our  mainstream universities where there's  this very you know anti-establishment  really anti-capitalist  anti-profit  all the things that pay for the  university  uh ideology  uh these students are there paying three  five thousand dollars a year  uh when the true cost is 30 000 they  feel that they should have to pay less  that everything should be provided for  them and they want to basically beat up  on the system that provides that pays  the taxes that pays for the universities  so and of course the professors are very  much involved with with uh  fomenting this kind of attitude so  you know i guess the the point of of all  of this is  um  how much you learn whether at a  university or not at a university  is largely dependent on how motivated  you are to read or nowadays on the  internet to access  people  videos other sources of knowledge about  the subject you're interested in  and i'm referring primarily to the  humanities to to  history literature languages uh  things of this nature  but let's not forget  that there are i think it's only 12  of students who study the hard subjects  you know math science engineering  and there i think the state should pick  up the tab because obviously for any  certainly developed economy that that  wants to be an innovative economy and a  competitive economy they need people  with those kinds of skills so there i  think the state should pick up more of  the tab but when it comes to the  humanities which is largely a matter of  reading because the reading will not  only enable you to access so much  knowledge  uh it's also going to improve your  ability to communicate which is  extremely important it's interesting in  this article now that i remember it they  found that students who study on their  own learn more  students who spend most of their time  studying in groups don't learn very much  i've always felt that i've never quite  understood this desire to get people  together in groups to learn  you kind of have to sit down with the  book and read  and in the case of language learning  i've always felt  that  the time that i spend listening and  reading on my own is much more valuable  and much more efficient  uh than time that i spend with other  learners  so i mean you know my bent on this i'm  very much of a of an in pro independent  learning uh and and particularly if i'm  paying for it  somehow in there we should be  encouraging people to read more  and and making people more responsible  for their own learning so there you have  it a little bit of a departure from from  the language learning stuff  and i look forward to  your comments thank you for listening
 

